# Database

The server runs on PostgreSQL. Tenant isolation is enforced at the database layer with row-level security, so the server never serves traffic with one all-powerful login. You provide an external Postgres, migrate it once per release with the database-owner login, and then run the server with two narrower serving logins.

## Roles

Create the two serving roles before you migrate. `crates/wyrd/wyrd-sql/bootstrap/roles.sql` is the bootstrap template; run it with `psql` 15 or later as the database owner, passing both passwords in the environment variables `WYRD_APP_PASSWORD` and `WYRD_PLATFORM_ADMIN_PASSWORD` and the owner's in `PGPASSWORD`, never as command-line arguments.

| Role | Row-level security | What it does |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `wyrd_app` | enforced | All request traffic. Sees only its tenant's rows. |
| `wyrd_platform_admin` | bypasses RLS | Audited cross-tenant platform work and the Bifrost Iceberg catalog. |

Neither serving role can create or drop databases or run migrations. The owner login you migrate with needs `BYPASSRLS` (or superuser) and must never be given to a serving process.

## Migrations

Migrations are a separate one-off step, not part of server boot. Run it with `WYRD_DATABASE_URL` set to the database-owner login before starting or upgrading servers:

```bash
WYRD_DATABASE_URL="postgresql://owner:owner-pw@db.internal:5432/wyrd" wyrd-server migrate
```

It applies every Wyrd and Vala migration, validates the result, and exits. It is idempotent, so rerunning it after a partial failure is safe.

### When a migration fails

Take and verify a database backup before every upgrade; that verified backup is your recovery point. If `wyrd-server migrate` exits nonzero:

1. Stop the rollout. Servers that are still running keep serving, and new servers refuse to become ready against the incomplete schema.
2. Fix the cause the error names (permissions, disk, connectivity, or a conflicting object) and rerun `wyrd-server migrate`. Completed migrations are skipped and the failed one is retried.
3. If the database cannot be repaired, restore the verified backup and rerun the migration.

Wyrd has no down migrations and never restores automatically.

## Serving

A serving process receives exactly two URLs:

| Variable | Role | Purpose |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `WYRD_DATABASE_URL` | `wyrd_app` | Request traffic under row-level security. |
| `WYRD_PLATFORM_DATABASE_URL` | `wyrd_platform_admin` | Cross-tenant platform work and the Iceberg catalog. |

```bash
export WYRD_DATABASE_URL="postgresql://wyrd_app:app-pw@db.internal:5432/wyrd"
export WYRD_PLATFORM_DATABASE_URL="postgresql://wyrd_platform_admin:admin-pw@db.internal:5432/wyrd"
```

The server never runs DDL. Before it reports ready it checks that every migration it embeds is applied with a matching checksum, that each serving login has exactly its expected row-level-security posture, and that every tenant table forces row-level security. If any check fails, boot stops with `WYRD_SQL_503_SCHEMA_NOT_READY`; run `wyrd-server migrate` and start again.

### Pool tuning

Pool settings are covered in [Configuration](/self-hosting/configuration/): `WYRD_PG_POOL_MAX` (default 32) and `WYRD_PG_POOL_ACQUIRE_MS` (default 5000). Size your cluster's `max_connections` above the sum of the app, Vala, and platform-admin pools across replicas.

